---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../components/ui/Button.astro";
import Divider from "../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import officerPhoto from "../assets/about/officer-sia-badge.png";

/**
 * About page — REDESIGN PASS (first build was PR #38; this is a direct
 * follow-up visual-weight revision, not a rewrite of the content). The
 * first build read "flat and thin" on live review: four stacked plain
 * sections, a middle-dot separated service list (the generic-AI tell
 * the original brief was built to avoid), and body text sized larger
 * than the rest of the site. This pass restructures the page into two
 * dense, deliberate full-bleed bands instead of four thin ones — same
 * content, same photo, same CTA link, genuinely different visual
 * weight.
 *
 * OPENING — Magenta full-bleed, NOT the Ink/dark band the first
 * revision plan proposed. Explicitly overruled by direct instruction:
 * no dark section on this page. Magenta was chosen over plain Paper
 * (the other offered option) because plain Paper is what shipped
 * originally and is exactly what was flagged as reading flat — bigger
 * type alone doesn't fix that, it needs a real colour anchor. Magenta
 * carries the strongest text pairing on the whole site (white ~7.26:1,
 * AAA — already-derived, no new maths) and had never been used as a
 * full-bleed SECTION fill anywhere before this (only ever a card fill
 * in `ServicesGrid.astro`), so it also gives this page its own visual
 * identity rather than repeating Electric Blue twice in a row.
 * `ServicesGrid.astro` already proves Magenta sits fine directly next
 * to Electric Blue (its own Close Protection/Manned Guarding cards sit
 * adjacent) — this page just does the same pairing at full-bleed
 * section scale instead of card scale.
 * `.on-dark` is applied defensively — no focusable element in this
 * section (no button, just text + the photo), matching `ServicesGrid`
 * 's own precedent for a fill with no interactive content.
 *
 * CONTENT — unchanged from PR #38, still verbatim per the user's
 * original brief. No copy, photo, or CTA link edited in this pass —
 * pure visual-weight/layout revision.
 *
 * IMAGE — same asset, same crop, same alt text as PR #38
 * (`src/assets/about/officer-sia-badge.png`, real 2:3 geometry,
 * `object-cover` performs no actual crop).
 *
 * SERVICES LIST — the middle-dot inline separators are gone. Replaced
 * with hairline-divided rows, the SAME visual idiom already used for
 * the three-bullet fact list in the opening section (`divide-y` +
 * `border-t`, translucent Paper border rather than the `hairline`
 * token since this now sits on a colour fill) — one consistent
 * "divided list" pattern reused in two places, not two different list
 * styles invented for two sections.
 *
 * MISSION / SERVICES / VISION — merged into ONE full-bleed Electric
 * Blue band, laid out as three columns divided by `Divider.astro`'s
 * existing `paper-strong` tone. This is StatStrip's own already-built,
 * already-contrast-verified three-column-with-dividers mechanism
 * (`flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-stretch`, vertical `Divider` at
 * `sm:`+, horizontal `<hr>`-style `Divider` on mobile, `my-8` margin
 * doing mobile spacing instead of a flex gap) — reused wholesale
 * rather than inventing a new bordered-card treatment, which would
 * also risk reading as a fourth card-grid pattern on top of
 * `ServicesGrid.astro`'s already-sanctioned one. A plain divided
 * column row isn't a card grid at all (no border/shadow/bg per item),
 * so it sits outside that hard rule entirely, same as StatStrip
 * already does. `paper-strong` was previously calibrated with StatStrip
 * documented as "the sole real call site" — this page is now a SECOND
 * real call site for the exact same Electric Blue fill, so no new
 * contrast maths needed, and `Divider.astro`'s own comment is updated
 * in this same commit so it doesn't keep claiming "sole."
 *
 * `.on-dark` is DELIBERATELY OMITTED from this section, unlike the
 * Magenta opening above — a real, checked reason, not an oversight:
 * the Vision column holds a genuine focusable `Button` (Amber fill,
 * Ink text). `.on-dark`'s `:focus-visible` descendant selector would
 * force that button's ring to Paper, which fails outright against
 * Amber (~2.15:1, below the 3:1 floor) — the exact bug
 * `SectorsGrid.astro`'s own coloured content panels already found and
 * deliberately avoid for the identical reason (a focusable Amber
 * button inside a colour-filled section). Without `.on-dark`, the
 * button keeps the sitewide default Ink ring, ~9.05:1 against Amber,
 * already safe — matching every other Amber button on the site
 * regardless of what section colour surrounds it.
 *
 * TYPE — every running paragraph on the page drops from `text-body-lg`
 * (20px) to `text-body` (17px), matching `MissionBand.astro`'s own
 * established body-copy tier rather than the larger size PR #38
 * originally used. One exception: the hero subhead directly under the
 * H1 keeps `text-body-lg` — that's the token's own documented role
 * ("intro paragraphs, pull-quotes"), a legitimate different use, not
 * an inconsistency.
 *
 * HEADLINE — `text-h1 sm:text-display` (56px mobile → 88px desktop).
 * `text-display` is an existing, currently-unused-anywhere-else token
 * (`Hero.astro`'s own H1 was stepped DOWN to `text-h3 sm:text-h2` a
 * while back, per that file's own history) — reusing it here rather
 * than inventing a new size, and it doesn't compete with anything else
 * on the site since nothing else currently uses it.
 *
 * MOTION — still exactly one deliberate moment, same restraint as
 * PR #38's own brief. The scroll reveal (`[data-reveal]`, plain
 * `scrollReveal.ts`, unmodified) now lives on the Electric Blue
 * triptych band; the Magenta opening is static — it's the first thing
 * in view on load, the same "already in view, no scroll trigger
 * needed" logic `Hero.astro` already follows for its own load-visible
 * content.
 *
 * ALIGNMENT — left throughout, unchanged from PR #38.
 *
 * STRUCTURE — two full-bleed sections (Magenta opening, Electric Blue
 * triptych) plus Footer, down from PR #38's four stacked Paper/
 * Electric-Blue sections. Fewer, denser, more deliberate sections is
 * the actual fix for "reads flat and thin," not added decoration on
 * top of the same four-section structure.
 *
 * CTA TARGET — unchanged, `/#quote-form` (ClosingCta's own quote card,
 * anchor added in PR #38's own follow-up commit).
 */
const SERVICES = [
  "Corporate security",
  "Close protection",
  "CCTV monitoring",
  "Manned guarding",
  "Retail security",
  "Construction site security",
  "Overnight security",
  "Private property security",
  "Event stewarding",
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="House of Guards is a young, Bradford-based physical security company founded in 2023 — SIA-licensed, DBS-checked officers delivering manned guarding, close protection, and CCTV monitoring across West Yorkshire."
>
  <section class="on-dark bg-magenta">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-gutter py-20 md:px-12 md:py-28">
      <h1 class="text-h1 sm:text-display text-paper max-w-3xl">About House of Guards</h1>
      <p class="text-body-lg text-paper mt-4 max-w-2xl">A young company with experienced people</p>

      <div class="mt-12 grid grid-cols-1 items-start gap-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:gap-16 lg:gap-20">
        <div class="text-left">
          <p class="text-body text-paper max-w-md">
            House of Guards is a new establishment formed in 2023, working across the
            sectors listed on our Services page. We are growing steadily in both staff
            and contracts, and we continue to recruit skilled SIA licensed officers,
            CCTV monitoring operators and stewards.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body text-paper mt-5 max-w-md">
            Most of our work takes place in West Yorkshire, though some shifts run
            further afield with transport provided. Our staff receive regular training
            on how to sharpen their work, because professionalism is central to how we
            operate — and to what we expect from every member of the crew.
          </p>

          <ul class="mt-8 max-w-md divide-y divide-paper/15 border-t border-paper/15">
            <li class="py-4 text-body text-paper">SIA licensed and DBS checked officers</li>
            <li class="py-4 text-body text-paper">First aid trained personnel</li>
            <li class="py-4 text-body text-paper">Bespoke shift patterns, arranged around you</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="aspect-[2/3] overflow-hidden md:max-w-sm md:justify-self-end">
          <Image
            src={officerPhoto}
            alt="A House of Guards security officer in a branded jacket, wearing an SIA licence badge on a lanyard, standing on a Bradford street"
            class="h-full w-full object-cover"
            widths={[320, 480, 640, 768]}
            sizes="(min-width: 768px) 24rem, 100vw"
            format="webp"
            quality={82}
            loading="eager"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bg-electric-blue" data-reveal>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-gutter py-20 md:px-12 md:py-24">
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-0 lg:flex-row lg:items-stretch lg:gap-0">
        <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col text-left">
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-paper">Our mission</h2>
          <p class="text-body text-paper mt-4 max-w-sm">
            To meet our clients' needs to perfection in a professional working
            environment, and to recruit staff who share those values. We aim to give
            full assurance in the safety our guards provide, and to become a leading
            supplier of reliable close protection officers in the North.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lg:hidden">
          <Divider orientation="horizontal" tone="paper-strong" class="my-8" />
        </div>
        <div class="hidden shrink-0 lg:flex">
          <Divider orientation="vertical" tone="paper-strong" class="mx-10" />
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col text-left">
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-paper">Our services</h2>
          <p class="text-body text-paper mt-4 max-w-sm">We deliver:</p>
          <ul class="mt-3 max-w-sm divide-y divide-paper/15 border-t border-paper/15" aria-label="Services we offer">
            {SERVICES.map((service) => (
              <li class="py-2.5 text-body text-paper">{service}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="text-body text-paper mt-4 max-w-sm">
            Bespoke arrangements are available, and our specialists are operational
            24 hours a day.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lg:hidden">
          <Divider orientation="horizontal" tone="paper-strong" class="my-8" />
        </div>
        <div class="hidden shrink-0 lg:flex">
          <Divider orientation="vertical" tone="paper-strong" class="mx-10" />
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-1 flex-col text-left">
          <h2 class="text-h3 text-paper">Our vision</h2>
          <p class="text-body text-paper mt-4 max-w-sm">
            To offer the best quality for the right price. We invest in our people so
            they can outperform the rest, commit to ongoing improvement in every area,
            and build a team of experts who understand our core values and want to
            deliver on them.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-6">
            <Button href="/#quote-form">Get in touch</Button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    import { initScrollReveal } from "../lib/scrollReveal";

    document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", () => {
      initScrollReveal();
    });
  </script>
</BaseLayout>
